meta {
  name: Create Driver
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{plutusBaseUrl}}/api/drivers
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "name": "{{newDriverName}}",
    "phone": "{{newDriverPhone}}",
    "licenseNumber": "{{newDriverLicenseNumber}}",
    "email": "{{newDriverEmail}}",
    "ownerId": {{newDriverOwnerId}}
  }
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("createdDriverId", res.body.data.id);
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.success: eq true
  res.body.data.name: eq {{newDriverName}}
  res.body.data.phone: eq "{{newDriverPhone}}"
}

docs {
  Creates a driver directly as an admin/fleet role (`DriverResource.createDriver`,
  `common/controller/DriverResource.java:199-231`, service `DriverService.createDriver`).

  Roles: VAHN_ADMIN, TORG_ADMIN, TRUCK_MANAGER, FUND_MANAGER.

  `name` and `phone` (exactly 10 digits) are required; `licenseNumber` and `email` are optional
  and shown here as the common extra fields other driver requests in this collection populate.

  Ownership: for TORG_ADMIN/TRUCK_MANAGER/FUND_MANAGER callers, the driver is attached to the
  caller's own torg and `ownerId` in the body is ignored. For a VAHN_ADMIN caller, `ownerId` is
  the only source of the torg — omit or send a bad id and the driver is created with no owner
  (`torgDAO.findById` just returns empty, no error). Set `newDriverOwnerId` to a real torg id
  before running as VAHN_ADMIN; leave it unset (and drop the field) when running as a torg role.

  Every response is HTTP 200 with the real code in body `statusCode`, even for errors — this
  matches the other reference requests in this collection; check the body on failure.
  `DriverResource.createDriver` itself also still returns a raw 201 on success before the
  wrapper rewrites the status to 200, and a raw 400 (`{"message": ...}`) if `driverService
  .createDriver` throws `BadRequestException`.

  409/400 "Driver already exists" if a driver with this phone already has an owner (see
  `driver-onboarding`'s step 11 "Create Second Driver" for the same call from inside a flow,
  which needs a phone unique across the whole staging DB). If a driver with this phone exists
  but has no owner yet (e.g. self-registered, never linked), this call attaches it to the
  caller's/`ownerId`'s torg instead of creating a new row, and `verifyDLMatch` 400s if the
  incoming `licenseNumber` conflicts with the existing one.

  Verified on staging 2026-10-21: a reused `licenseNumber` (unlike a reused `phone`) is **not**
  caught by `DriverService.createDriver` before insert — it hits the DB's unique constraint and
  comes back as an unhandled `500` (`{"statusCode":500,"success":false,"message":"There was an
  error processing your request. Log ID: ..."}`), not the `400`/`409` the Swagger doc on this
  endpoint promises. Bump `newDriverLicenseNumber` to a fresh value before every re-run, same as
  `newDriverPhone`, or drop the field — it's optional.

  Set `accessToken` in the environment to a VAHN_ADMIN/TORG_ADMIN/TRUCK_MANAGER/FUND_MANAGER
  token first (e.g. run "Send OTP"/"Verify OTP" in the auth/ folder, or copy it from a flow run).
  Uses `{{plutusBaseUrl}}` rather than this collection's `{{baseUrl}}` — same staging host today,
  kept separate so this request doesn't silently move if `{{baseUrl}}` is ever repointed for the
  other requests in this collection.
}
